/** @page interruptible_io_module_guide Interruptible I/O in Binary Modules

    @tableofcontents

    Copyright 2026 Qore Technologies, s.r.o.

    This guide tells binary module authors how to make a module's blocking operations end as soon as the thread
    running them is cancelled (@ref Qore::cancel_thread() "cancel_thread()" or the C++ function
    <tt>qore_cancel_thread()</tt>) or its @ref Qore::Program "Program" is interrupted
    (@ref Qore::SandboxManager::requestInterrupt() "SandboxManager::requestInterrupt()"), and how to keep the
    module inside a @ref sandboxing "sandbox".  The design behind it is in \c design/cooperative-cancellation.md in
    the source tree; the sandboxing rules for modules are in \c design/module-sandboxing-audit-guide.md.

    @section interruptible_io_rule The Rule

    Every blocking wait must end as soon as cancellation is requested, without a periodic timeout:

    |!Wait|!Use
    |on descriptors the module owns|<tt>qore_cancellable_poll()</tt>
    |in a library's own event loop (epoll, kqueue, select, a library poll function)|add the descriptor from \
        <tt>qore_cancel_wakeup_register()</tt>
    |inside a library that exposes no descriptor and no event loop, but offers a cancel call|a cancel callback \
        (<tt>QoreSandboxManagerHelper::registerCancelCallback()</tt>)
    |on a <tt>QoreCondition</tt>|<tt>QoreCondition::waitWithInterrupt()</tt>
    |CPU-bound loops|<tt>qore_check_cancel()</tt> every 100 iterations (every 10 for expensive ones)

    Polling in <tt>QORE_IO_POLL_INTERVAL_MS</tt> slices is deprecated: it delays every cancellation by up to
    500 ms and wakes every waiting thread twice a second.

    All of these APIs are declared in <tt>qore/qore_thread.h</tt>, included by <tt>qore/Qore.h</tt>; the
    descriptor APIs are available when <tt>_QORE_HAS_CANCELLABLE_POLL</tt> is defined (%Qore 3.0 and later):

    @code{.cpp}
#ifdef _QORE_HAS_CANCELLABLE_POLL
    // deterministic wait
#else
    // older libqore: poll in QORE_IO_POLL_INTERVAL_MS slices with qore_check_cancel()
#endif
    @endcode

    @section interruptible_io_check Checking for a Request

    <tt>qore_check_cancel(xsink, "operation")</tt> returns \c true and raises \c THREAD-CANCELLED or
    \c PROGRAM-INTERRUPTED if a request is pending.  Call it before starting a blocking operation and in long loops.
    It costs one atomic load when nothing is pending.

    @section interruptible_io_poll Waiting on Descriptors: qore_cancellable_poll()

    @code{.cpp}
int qore_cancellable_poll(struct pollfd* fds, unsigned nfds, int timeout_ms, ExceptionSink* xsink,
    const char* operation = "poll");
    @endcode

    It waits like <tt>poll(2)</tt> with the thread's cancellation wakeup channel added to the descriptors, so a
    request ends the wait at once - also a request made just before the wait starts.

    |!Return Value|!Meaning
    |<tt>> 0</tt>|the number of descriptors in \c fds with events (\c revents is set)
    |\c 0|the timeout expired
    |\c -1|<tt>poll(2)</tt> failed: \c errno is set and no exception has been raised (\c EINTR is retried \
        internally for the rest of the timeout)
    |<tt>QORE_POLL_CANCELLED</tt>|an exception has been raised: \c THREAD-CANCELLED, \c PROGRAM-INTERRUPTED, or \
        \c THREAD-ERROR if the wakeup channel could not be created

    @code{.cpp}
int my_read(int fd, void* buf, size_t len, int timeout_ms, ExceptionSink* xsink) {
    struct pollfd pfd = {fd, POLLIN, 0};
    int rc = qore_cancellable_poll(&pfd, 1, timeout_ms, xsink, "reading the response");
    if (rc == QORE_POLL_CANCELLED) {
        return -1;
    }
    if (rc < 0) {
        xsink->raiseErrnoException("MY-READ-ERROR", errno, "poll() failed");
        return -1;
    }
    if (!rc) {
        xsink->raiseException("MY-TIMEOUT", "no response within %d ms", timeout_ms);
        return -1;
    }
    return read(fd, buf, len);
}
    @endcode

    A timeout of \c -1 waits without a timeout and \c 0 checks without waiting; \c nfds may be \c 0 for a
    cancellable sleep.  While cancellation is deferred (<tt>QoreCancelDeferralHelper</tt>) and on a thread that is
    not registered with %Qore, nothing can be delivered and the call is a plain <tt>poll(2)</tt>.

    When the function is called from a library callback that can only report \c errno, clear the exception and
    return \c EINTR; the request stays pending and is raised at the module's next <tt>qore_check_cancel()</tt>:

    @code{.cpp}
ExceptionSink xsink;
int rc = qore_cancellable_poll(&pfd, 1, timeout_ms, &xsink, "library read");
if (rc == QORE_POLL_CANCELLED) {
    xsink.clear();
    errno = EINTR;
    return -1;
}
    @endcode

    @section interruptible_io_event_loop A Library's Own Event Loop: the Wakeup Descriptor

    @code{.cpp}
int qore_cancel_wakeup_register(ExceptionSink* xsink, const char* operation = "wait");
int qore_cancel_wakeup_check(ExceptionSink* xsink, const char* operation = "wait");
void qore_cancel_wakeup_unregister();
class QoreCancelWakeupHelper;   // RAII for register/unregister
    @endcode

    -# Register before the wait.  The call returns the descriptor to watch; <tt>QORE_CANCEL_WAKEUP_NONE</tt> if
       there is nothing to watch (the thread is not registered with %Qore, so it cannot be cancelled); or \c -1 with
       the exception raised if a request was already pending.
    -# Add the descriptor to the loop <b>for readability only</b> (\c POLLIN, \c EPOLLIN, \c EVFILT_READ, a
       <tt>select()</tt> read set).  Never read, write or close it.  On macOS it is a kqueue, which
       <tt>poll()</tt>, <tt>select()</tt> and another kqueue can watch.
    -# When the descriptor is readable, call <tt>qore_cancel_wakeup_check()</tt>: it returns \c -1 with the
       exception raised (end the wait), or \c 0 if there is nothing to deliver (continue waiting; the descriptor is
       no longer readable, so the loop does not spin).
    -# After the wait, whatever ended it, unregister - <tt>QoreCancelWakeupHelper</tt> does it in its destructor.

    @code{.cpp}
QoreCancelWakeupHelper cwh(xsink, "waiting for the broker");
if (*xsink) {
    return -1;
}
if (cwh.fd() >= 0) {
    my_loop_watch_read(loop, cwh.fd());
}
while (true) {
    int ready_fd = my_loop_wait(loop, timeout_ms);
    if (ready_fd == cwh.fd()) {
        if (qore_cancel_wakeup_check(xsink, "waiting for the broker")) {
            return -1;
        }
        continue;
    }
    // handle the event
}
    @endcode

    There is no race window: registering checks for a request after storing the registration, so a request made at
    any time either is raised by the registration or makes the descriptor readable; the check drains the descriptor
    before it checks, so a request made after the drain makes it readable again.  Registrations nest, and
    <tt>qore_cancellable_poll()</tt> can be called while one is active.

    @section interruptible_io_transport Libraries that Let the Module Supply the Transport

    If a library takes custom streams or I/O callbacks (custom stream initiators and stream vtables, OpenSSL BIOs,
    socket callbacks), let the module own the socket, make it non-blocking, and wait with
    <tt>qore_cancellable_poll()</tt> in the callbacks.  When the library can only report \c errno from a callback,
    clear the exception and report \c EINTR as shown above.  The @ref mongodbintro "mongodb" module
    (\c modules/mongodb/src/QoreMongoStream.cpp in the source tree) is the reference: it connects its own socket in
    its libmongoc stream initiator, implements a libmongoc stream on it, and lets libmongoc stack its TLS stream on
    top.

    @section interruptible_io_no_hooks Libraries without Hooks

    A library that blocks on descriptors it does not expose can only be stopped by its own cancel call: register a
    cancel callback with <tt>QoreSandboxManagerHelper::registerCancelCallback()</tt> (for example
    <tt>PQcancel()</tt>, <tt>OCIBreak()</tt>, <tt>sqlite3_interrupt()</tt> or <tt>SQLCancel()</tt>).  The
    callback is called for a program interrupt; for thread cancellation, check with <tt>qore_check_cancel()</tt>
    before and after the call.  Only when no such call exists, poll in <tt>QORE_IO_POLL_INTERVAL_MS</tt> slices, and
    document the delay.

    @section interruptible_io_cleanup Cleanup that Must Complete

    Cleanup that must run on a cancelled thread (releasing a lock it owns, closing a connection) runs inside a
    <tt>QoreCancelDeferralHelper</tt>; no cancellation is delivered while it is active, and the pending request is
    raised at the first cancellation point after it.

    @section interruptible_io_fork Child Processes Created with fork()

    A child process created with \c fork() runs only the thread that called \c fork().  A module registers fork
    handlers for its process-wide locks with <tt>qore_register_fork_handler()</tt>, and a module whose third-party
    library has exit-time code that is not safe in such a child (an \c atexit() handler or static destructor that
    joins the library's threads, takes a lock that one of them held, or destroys a kernel object that the child does
    not have) calls <tt>qore_fork_child_exit_quickly()</tt> from its child handler, so that the child ends without
    running exit-time handlers.  Both are described in the module guide of the C++ API documentation ("Process-Wide
    Locks and fork()" and "Exit-Time Code of Third-Party Libraries in a Child Process") and in
    <tt>include/qore/QoreForkHandler.h</tt>.

    @section interruptible_io_sandboxing Sandboxing

    Check every filesystem and network access against the program's sandbox before making it, and mark functions
    and methods with their functional domains in \c .qpp files (\c dom=FILESYSTEM, \c dom=NETWORK,
    \c dom=PROCESS, ...), so that a @ref Qore::Program "Program"'s restrictions are enforced.

    @subsection interruptible_io_sandbox_threads Carrying the Caller's Sandbox onto Another Thread

    A thread started with <tt>q_start_thread()</tt> or the \c background operator inherits the
    @ref sandboxing "sandbox" (the \c QoreSandboxManager, carrying filesystem and network policy, program
    interrupts, the cancellation scope and the thread limit) of the code that starts it, applied as the outermost
    caller of everything the thread runs.  So a module that starts one thread for one unit of work for one caller
    needs to do nothing: the thread is already governed by that caller's sandbox, and work it runs in a
    @ref Qore::Program "Program" that has a sandbox of its own is governed by that one, exactly as in a
    synchronous call.

    A thread that runs work for <b>many</b> callers - a worker pool, or a thread that a native library owns and
    that runs callbacks registered by different callers (a message-delivery thread, an event or notification
    callback, an async-completion callback) - must instead govern each piece of work by the sandbox of the code
    that submitted or registered it.  Such a thread opts out of thread-start inheritance with
    \c QTF_NO_SANDBOX_INHERIT, and for each unit of work:

    - captures the submitting thread's sandbox with \c QoreSandboxCapture <b>when the work is submitted or the
      callback is registered</b> (in the submitting thread), stored next to the callback and its \c QoreProgram;
    - applies it with \c QoreSandboxCaptureHelper <b>around the work</b> on the worker or callback thread.

    Both are declared in \c qore/QoreSandboxManager.h.  \c QoreSandboxCapture is immutable after construction and
    may be shared between threads; a capture of an unsandboxed submitter applies as "no sandbox", so the pattern is
    safe to use unconditionally.  It works on a foreign thread registered with \c q_register_foreign_thread() (or
    \c QoreForeignThreadHelper): register the thread, enter the callback's \c QoreProgram with
    \c QoreExternalProgramContextHelper, then apply the capture.  The core \c ThreadPool
    (\c include/qore/intern/ThreadPool.h) and the async I/O controller are the reference implementations.

    @code{.cpp}
    // when the caller registers the callback (in the caller's thread):
    class MyCallbackContext {
    public:
        ResolvedCallReferenceNode* callback;  // referenced
        QoreProgram* pgm;                      // the callback's program (dep-referenced)
        QoreSandboxCapture sandbox;            // captures the registering caller's sandbox here
    };

    // on the native/foreign thread the library calls back on:
    extern "C" void my_library_callback(void* arg, const char* data) {
        MyCallbackContext* ctx = static_cast<MyCallbackContext*>(arg);
        QoreForeignThreadHelper fth;           // register this foreign thread with Qore (no-op if already a Qore thread)
        if (!fth) {
            return;
        }
        ExceptionSink xsink;
        QoreExternalProgramContextHelper pch(&xsink, ctx->pgm);
        if (xsink) {
            xsink.clear();
            return;
        }
        QoreSandboxCaptureHelper sch(ctx->sandbox);  // the callback now runs under the registering caller's sandbox
        ReferenceHolder<QoreListNode> args(new QoreListNode(autoTypeInfo), &xsink);
        args->push(new QoreStringNode(data), &xsink);
        ctx->callback->execValue(*args, &xsink).discard(&xsink);
        xsink.handleExceptions();
    }
    @endcode

    See <tt>design/thread-sandbox-inheritance.md</tt> for the full model.

    @subsection interruptible_io_sandbox_pools Reusing Connections for Other Callers

    A connection is checked against the network sandbox of the code that makes it.  A module that keeps open
    connections and hands them to later callers - a connection pool, or a client object that callers share - must
    check each connection against the network sandbox of every caller it hands the connection to, as a new
    connection would be checked: record the host name and the address that the connection was made to (and, for a
    connection through a proxy, the destination that the proxy connects to), and before reusing the connection for
    a caller, check them with the policy of that caller.  If the sandbox denies them, make a new connection for the
    caller instead, which the sandbox decides on as usual.

    @code{.cpp}
    // before a pooled connection is handed to the calling code:
    QoreSandboxManagerHelper smh(QoreSandboxManagerHelper::Policy);
    if (smh && !smh->checkNetworkAccess(conn->host.c_str(), reinterpret_cast<const struct sockaddr*>(&conn->addr),
            conn->addrlen, QSEC_NET_TCP, xsink)) {
        // the sandbox denies this connection to the calling code and raised NETWORK-ACCESS-DENIED in xsink:
        // clear it and make a new connection for the caller, or report it if no other connection can be made
        ...
    }
    @endcode

    Check on every reuse rather than once per caller: a sandbox can be tightened while the connection stays open.
    See <tt>design/pooled-connection-sandbox.md</tt> for how the HTTP client pools of %Qore do this.

    @section interruptible_io_testing Testing

    - A cancellation test blocks a thread in the wait, cancels it, and checks that the call ends with
      \c THREAD-CANCELLED well under the old polling interval - deterministically: make the request only once the
      thread is known to be in (or about to enter) the wait, and use a wait without a timeout, so that a missed
      wakeup can never end it.
    - A request made before the wait must be raised at once.
    - Without a request, the wait must return the same results as before (readiness, timeouts).
    - \c examples/test/module-cpp-api/cancellable-poll/cancellable-poll.qtest in the source tree exercises both
      APIs from a test module's C++ code.

    @since %Qore 3.0
*/
